# Writer Publication

`writer-publication.ts` owns `publishWriterArtifact()`, the one function every other module uses to put
a byte on disk durably. It is the shared persistence core, and a change here affects everything.

## Nine consumers

| Module | Publishes |
|---|---|
| `writer-publication.ts` | owner |
| `writer-entrypoint.ts` | the public writer seam, and the admission-relevant one |
| `async-worker-carrier.ts` | worker transaction state and results |
| `async-job-lifecycle.ts` | seals and CAS lifecycle events |
| `async-admission-facade.ts` | the immutable ownership receipt |
| `async-control-plane.ts` | projected queue and redrive refs |
| `async-progress-store.ts` | worker progress |
| `evidence-cost-cache.ts` | canonical requests and cache entries |
| `evidence-cost-collector.ts` | request, execution, observation and completion receipts |

The entrypoint and the worker are material boundaries in their own right — the first is the public
seam, the second publishes results from a subprocess-bearing transaction — and neither is "just another
async module".

## Why it is not just `writeFile`

Every guarantee below exists because a crashed or racing writer must never leave a half-published
artifact that a later reader treats as complete.

- **Directory-relative syscalls via Bun FFI** — `openat`, `linkat`, `unlinkat`, `flock`. Paths are
  resolved once against an open directory handle rather than re-resolved per operation, so a directory
  swapped underneath the writer cannot redirect the write.
- **`O_NOFOLLOW`** — a symlink in the target position is an error, not a redirect.
- **Root and parent inode identity** — the identity of the directories is verified, not just their
  names, closing the rename-the-directory race.
- **Pending hard link then `fsync`** — content is written and durably flushed to a pending name, then
  `linkat` publishes it. Publication is a single atomic link, so a reader sees the final name only when
  the bytes are already durable.
- **Publish-readback** — after linking, the artifact is reopened and verified. A publish that cannot be
  read back is a failure, not a success with a warning.
- **Pre-link and post-link crash recovery** — a crash before the link leaves an orphan pending file that
  is reclaimed; a crash after the link leaves a complete artifact plus a stale pending name, which is
  cleaned without touching the published bytes.
- **`matched-existing` versus `conflict`** — republishing identical bytes to an existing name is
  idempotent success; different bytes to the same name is a typed conflict. Idempotent republication is
  what makes the two-admitter convergence in
  [async lifecycle](async-lifecycle.md) safe.
- **Aggregated cleanup failure** — cleanup errors are collected and surfaced as a terminal failure
  rather than being swallowed. `SKILL.md` states the rule for the whole operator: *"Treat cleanup
  failures as terminal failures, not log-only warnings."*

## Failure taxonomy

Failures are typed and distinguished, because the recovery differs:

| Class | Meaning |
|---|---|
| precondition | the root, parent or lease was not what the request bound |
| conflict | the name exists with different bytes |
| integrity | readback did not match what was published |
| cleanup | the artifact published but cleanup failed — still terminal |
| system | a syscall failed; the original diagnostic is preserved |

`SKILL.md`: *"No empty catch or default-value fallback after a system failure. Preserve the original
diagnostic and emit a typed nonzero failure receipt."*

## Probes

The behaviors that only appear under stress have dedicated scenario modules:
`writer-production-race-scenario.ts`, `writer-production-scenarios.ts`,
`writer-production-observer.ts`, `writer-production-fixture.ts`, and
`writer-publication-probe.ts` / `writer-publication-read.ts` /
`writer-publication-lifecycle.ts` / `writer-publication-contract.ts`. These cover concurrent
publication, kill-mid-publish, and cleanup-failure paths.

**They cannot run here.** There is no Bun toolchain, no `package.json`, and the contained production
profile they need lives in an absent upstream tree — see
[Terminal operator overview](overview.md) and
[Production profiles and handoff](production-profiles-and-handoff.md).

## If you change this file

Every consumer above inherits the change. The narrowest meaningful check is the writer's own contract
and probe modules, followed by the production profile that exercises the real carrier — not a unit test
of a single caller.
