# Terminal Operator Overview

`.agents/skills/repo-terminal-operator/` is ~9.7k lines of Bun TypeScript implementing a **leased
terminal-slice implementer**: an agent that is handed one bounded slice of work, verifies it may touch
it, does it, and returns a typed receipt. It is invoked when `repo-neural-perception` delegates a
`terminal-slice-packet@v2` to this repository.

## Three tiers of what is actually here

Read this first; it determines what any other statement on these pages can mean.

**Vendored and present** — 50 `.ts` modules, three profiles (`code-quality.profile.json`,
`production-use.profile.json`, `production-use` variants), `generated-core/manifest.json`, and
`SKILL.md`.

**Required but absent from this checkout** — `runtime/`, `skills/repo-neural-perception/` (which owns
the `production-use.md` SSOT and `writer-contained-production-profile.ts`), adapter configuration,
schemas, and the Bun test suites `task-quality-contract.ts` references. There is also **no
`package.json`, no `tsconfig.json`, and no local TypeScript test** in this tree.

**Consequence** — this copy cannot be executed or verified standalone. Nothing on these pages is
evidence produced here; it is contract read from vendored source. External test suites must not be cited
as evidence available in this repository.

## Contracts

| Direction | Contract |
|---|---|
| input | `terminal-slice-packet@v2` (v1 retained only for backward-compatible preflight) |
| output | `small-loop-run-receipt@v1` |
| description | `repo-terminal-operator-description@v1` |
| preflight | `repo-terminal-preflight-receipt@v1` |

A v2 packet must hash-bind its complete `guided-claim-set@v1`. Commands are run as argv arrays; a packet
is never interpolated into a shell string.

## CLI surface

`repo-adapter.ts` is the entrypoint, resolving `outputRepo` two levels above the module directory and
`workspaceRoot` two above that:

```sh
bun run repo-adapter.ts --describe            # profile summary
bun run repo-adapter.ts --selftest            # same, but fails if either profile has no real command
bun run repo-adapter.ts --preflight <packet>  # lease/HEAD/path gate only
bun run repo-adapter.ts --run <packet>        # preflight -> code-quality -> production-use -> receipt
```

`--run` blocks production-use after a code-quality failure, so the two profiles are ordered but not
merged. Exit codes: `0` passed, `2` failed, `64` usage.

## Preflight

`repo-preflight.ts` gates before any edit. It verifies the objective, invariants, public contract,
failure modes, bounded active files, red/green receipts, minimal-diff assertions, target repository,
expected Git head, live write lease, allowed paths, and the generated-core bundle. Two exported checks
are named directly: `claimSetCheck()` (the v2 hash-bound claim set) and `tddReceiptCheck()` (a failing
public-behavior test must exist before implementation).

The operator may modify only `allowed_paths`, only while the expected HEAD and the lease remain valid,
and must recheck both before publishing a candidate. Scope drift is a structured failure, not a
negotiation.

## Quality profiles are independent

`code-quality.profile.json` and `production-use.profile.json` run **independently**; neither substitutes
for the other, and a green test substitutes for neither.

`task-quality-contract.ts` is the registration chain for the task profile: four bounded static stages
run in a parallel phase — typed ESLint, Prettier check, strict TypeScript, dependency boundaries — and
the bounded focused behavior suites run **only when every static stage passes**. The contract's
sharpest rule: *exit code zero without an observed test count is a failure*. A suite that silently
selected nothing cannot report success.

The task profile deliberately records coverage as `not-selected` rather than claiming a misleading
aggregate over subprocess-tested modules, and routes coverage/race/leak admission to
`production-use/writer-entrypoint` instead. See
[Production profiles and handoff](production-profiles-and-handoff.md).

## What it may never do

From `SKILL.md`, and worth stating because several modules look like they could:

- Return `small-loop-run-receipt@v1` — but **not** admit, merge, release, or rewrite Git history.
- A preflight PASS emits the next writer-production mode; it **cannot** admit writer safety.
- The control plane may project queue and redrive refs but must not discover implicit work, execute
  workers, update `計畫.md`, change Forgejo, or admit a result.
- The evidence-cost projector never runs a worker or grants admission.
- The Forgejo seam pushes Git state but cannot create repositories, issues, PRs, or merges.

## Pages

- [Async lifecycle](async-lifecycle.md) — seal, projection, worker, admission, and what really executes.
- [Writer publication](writer-publication.md) — the atomic persistence core nine modules depend on.
- [Evidence cost](evidence-cost.md) — cost measurement and the unclosed v1/v2 handoff.
- [Production profiles and handoff](production-profiles-and-handoff.md) — admission-valid profiles and
  the Forgejo boundary.
